      "title": "Assistant Professor Social Work",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-30\n\nLocation: Lakewood\n\nThe Department of Social Work & Gerontology at Georgian Court University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position in the Social Work program. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching, scholarship, service, and program development in a CSWE-accredited program grounded in Mercy core values, social justice, and community engagement. Responsibilities Teaching & Instruction Teach BSW/ MSW courses (typical load: approximately 24 credit hours per academic year) Deliver instruction across foundation and advanced practice areas (e.g., HBSE, practice methods, policy, research, clinical practice, and specialization courses) Integrate evidence-based practice, cultural humility, and anti-oppressive frameworks into the curriculum Advise and mentor BSW/MSW students throughout their academic progression Scholarship & Professional Activity Maintain an active scholarly agenda through research, publications, presentations, or community-engaged scholarship Engage in professional development aligned with social work education and practice Contribute to program visibility and academic excellence Service & Program Support Participate in departmental, college, and university committees Support CSWE accreditation activities, assessment, and continuous program improvement Collaborate with faculty on curriculum design and review Engage in community partnerships and field education initiatives Qualifications Required PhD or DSW, and an MSW from a CSWE-accredited program Minimum of 2–5 years of post-MSW social work practice experience   Strong commitment to teaching and social work education Demonstrated commitment to social justice and culturally responsive practice Preferred New Jersey LSW or LCSW license, or eligibility for licensure in New Jersey.   Experience teaching at the graduate level Prior involvement in CSWE accreditation, assessment, or curriculum development **Must be US Work Authorized**",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=0674ba22-8f97-458a-80c0-91217826b19b&ccId=9151465763873_19553&jobId=9200925955766_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Associate Professor of Counseling",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-22\n\nLocation: Lakewood\n\nThe Clinical Mental Health Counseling graduate program in the Department of Psychology and Counseling at Georgian Court University is seeking an energetic and motivated faculty member to join our department for the Spring 2027 semester. This appointment is a full-time, tenure-track assistant professor position with primary assignment to our CACREP-accredited Clinical Mental Health Counseling (CMHC) graduate program (M.A. and Professional Certificate) with some undergraduate teaching responsibilities within the applicant's area(s) of expertise. Applicants with full-time faculty experience in a Clinical Mental Health Counseling graduate program with a desire and willingness to assume directorship of the GCU program are especially encouraged to apply for the associate professor position. Mentoring from the current program director will be provided. Primary responsibilities for this position will include teaching 24 credits per academic year (12 fall & 12 spring) with at least 75% of the candidate’s teaching load in the CMHC graduate program. Any remaining coursework outside of the CMHC graduate program will be in the undergraduate Psychology program. Course releases will be provided for a candidate who assumes the graduate program directorship. Scholarship, including publications and presentations at state and national conferences, along with service to the department, school, and university, are expected for tenure and promotion. The Department of Psychology and Counseling houses undergraduate programs leading to a B.A. in Psychology and a B.S. in Psychiatric Rehabilitation and Psychology (jointly with Rutgers University). We offer graduate programs in Applied Behavior Analysis (M.A.), Clinical Mental Health Counseling (M.A. and Professional Certificate), and School Psychology (M.A., Certificate of Advanced Graduate Study, and Psy.D.). The Department is currently comprised of 13 full-time, tenured or tenure-track faculty members who teach across the undergraduate and graduate programs, as well as a full-time field placement coordinator and a staff assistant. The Department of Psychology and Counseling is the largest unit in the School of Arts & Sciences to offer both undergraduate and graduate programs. Essential Functions: Integrate the Mercy core values of respect, integrity, compassion, justice, and service in all aspects of interaction; Teach across the curriculum in the Clinical Mental Health Counseling graduate program and courses aligned with the candidate’s specialty area(s) in the undergraduate Psychology program; Develop an active line of scholarship commensurate with a tenure-track position in a teaching university; Participate in department, school, and university service; Participate in ongoing (CACREP) accreditation activities for the Clinical Mental Health Counseling program; Advise/mentor students; Collaborate in program development, student recruitment, student remediation, and continuous program assessment and improvement; Regularly participate in course assessment and program assessment and be willing to make improvements in teaching/advising; Teach at the Lakewood campus, at off-site locations (if necessary), in the evening, and on weekends (if necessary); Participate in department functions such as information sessions, Open House, etc. Minimum Required Qualifications for Assistant Professor Rank (tenure-track) Earned a doctorate in Counselor Education. Candidates who are ABD will be considered if they can provide evidence that their doctorate will be completed no later than December 2027. Doctoral degrees in Psychology (Counseling, Clinical, School, etc.) will not be considered for this position due to CACREP accreditation requirements. Eligible for Licensed Professional Counselor (LPC) credential in New Jersey. Include a copy of LPC if applicable. Eligible for Approved Clinical Supervisor (ACS) credential. Include a copy of ACS if applicable. Potential for excellence in instruction at the university level. Potential for sustained scholarship production. Minimum Required Qualifications for Associate Professor Rank (tenure-track) Earned a doctorate in Counselor Education. Doctoral degrees in Psychology (Counseling, Clinical, School, etc.) will not be considered for this position due to CACREP accreditation requirements. Licensed Professional Counselor (LPC) credential in New Jersey. Include a copy of LPC if applicable. Eligible for Approved Clinical Supervisor (ACS) credential. Include a copy of ACS if applicable. Prior full-time teaching experience at the college or university level. Evidence of scholarly activity. Desire and willingness to assume directorship of the Clinical Mental Health Counseling graduate program. **Must be US Work Authorized** Include shift schedule Not Included Include budgeted hours Not Included",
      "department": ""
    },
